    <%# == HOW MUCH OF EACH AREA SPECGUARD CAN ACTUALLY READ
        The discharge of the one figure on the Overview panel that named a problem and offered no
        route into it. That panel used to print `total_specs_count - annotated_specs_count` as "Not
        visible to SpecGuard" and then say, in as many words, *"SpecGuard cannot see the other 12,403
        tests."* A subtraction was the entire answer it could give: a five-figure count, and not one
        path a reader could open. This panel is the rows behind it.

        SPGD-711 then corrected the claim on both surfaces. The subtraction counts the examples
        carrying no AUTHORED `@intent`, which is exact; SpecGuard nonetheless READS most of them,
        from the `Class#method behavior` description the client has been sending and this platform
        has been storing all along (see `DerivedIntent`). So each area is split three ways here —
        authored, derived, unreadable — and only the third is a count of tests the product may
        describe as ones it cannot see.

        The same rows have had a ranked, scoped worklist on the API since SPGD-591/608/623 and on
        the MCP bridge since SPGD-599. The dashboard — the surface the owner actually opens — was
        the rung that was never built, and it is the item SPGD-590 deferred by name.

        NOT the by-duration rollup directly above, which ranks the same population — one run's areas
        — on a different axis and cannot stand in for this one. That panel ranks by WALL CLOCK and
        its coverage column is TIMING coverage; an area of four hundred fast unannotated examples
        heads this list and appears nowhere near the head of that one. Two reads, side by side,
        each stating its own basis.

        Rendered whenever there is a RUN, and the three states it distinguishes are the whole point
        of the gating:

        * No run at all — no panel. The Overview's "No CI run has reported yet" is this page's one
          statement of that, and a second empty state repeating it would invite the reading that a
          repository CI has never reported for measured zero debt.
        * A run that wrote no per-example rows — the panel renders and says the DETAIL is missing.
          That is a fact about what the client sent, and it must not wear the spelling of "nothing
          is unannotated".
        * A run whose rows carry no debt — the panel renders and says so out loud. `RejectedIngests`
          states the rule this follows: a positive finding has to be distinguishable from "SpecGuard
          does not track that", and a panel that vanished when the news was good would leave the two
          looking identical.

        Outside the `manage_keys` gate, like the rankings above: read-only suite telemetry, nothing
        credential and nothing actionable on these rows.

        EACH AREA IS A LINK, and it was not. This panel shipped naming an area for an ask only the
        JSON API could answer — `UnannotatedDirectories.for` takes no narrowing argument, and there
        was no per-area rung beneath this one on the page — so a reader handed a ranking of where
        their debt was had to leave the product to see one test in it. There is a rung now:
        "Unannotated tests here", opened with `?spec_directory=`, which is the SAME ask the duration
        drill-downs read rather than a second parameter minted for this one. %>
    <% if @unannotated_directories %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "How SpecGuard reads this suite, by area",
                                        id: "unannotated-directories",
                                        class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
        <% if @unannotated_directories.recorded? %>
          <%# Whether any LISTED area carries debt — and the reason that is allowed to be read as a
              statement about the RUN, which for a capped list it usually is not.

              The ranking is unreadable-count DESC, then `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status =
              'unannotated') DESC`, so the maximum DEBT is still at the HEAD — which takes one step
              more to see than it used to and is still exact. If no area has an unreadable example
              the first term ties everywhere and the second orders the list, so the head carries the
              maximum debt outright. If some area does, that area's unreadable examples are
              unannotated ones by construction — a reading is derived or unreadable only when no
              `@intent` was authored — so the head carries debt too. Either way: head carries none ⇒
              no row below it and no row cut by the limit carries any.

              That inference is the only thing that makes a run-scoped sentence safe under a caption
              that says "the 10 of 84"; it is stated here rather than assumed, because the same fold
              under any OTHER ordering would be a claim about ten areas dressed as a claim about
              eighty.

              In the view and not on the object on purpose: `UnannotatedDirectories` ships operands
              and no verdicts, and "this run has debt" is exactly the kind of reading its class
              comment twice refuses to make on the reader's behalf. Nothing is recomputed here — the
              counts printed below are the object's own integers, and this asks only whether one of
              them is positive. %>
          <% carries_debt = @unannotated_directories.rows.any? { |directory| directory.unannotated_count.positive? } %>

          <% if carries_debt %>
            <%# What the rows ARE, before they are read as a verdict, and what the list is the head
                OF. Both claims are counted off the same grouped read that returned the rows, so this
                paragraph cannot end up describing a different row set from the table under it. %>
            <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="unannotated-directories-basis">
              <%# The cap, disclosed whether or not anything was cut. `rows.size` cannot state the
                  population — it IS the truncated figure — so the total rides back on the same
                  aggregate and `#truncated?` decides which sentence is true. A cut list that does
                  not say it was cut is read as the whole story. %>
              <% if @unannotated_directories.truncated? %>
                The <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_directories.rows.size) %> areas SpecGuard
                reads least of, of the
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_directories.directory_count) %> this run
                recorded examples in — most unreadable examples first, then most unannotated
              <% else %>
                All <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_directories.directory_count) %>
                <%= "directory".pluralize(@unannotated_directories.directory_count) %> this run
                recorded examples in — most unreadable examples first, then most unannotated
              <% end %>
              — grouped on the immediate parent of the file that RAN each example, so a shared
              example group counts against the area including it rather than against
              <code>spec/support</code>, and the areas sit side by side rather than inside one
              another.
              <%# The denominator is on every row for the same reason the panel refuses a fraction:
                  the two operands are what a reader decides FROM. Said here as well because a reader
                  scanning a column of counts needs to know what the second one is before the first
                  means anything. %>
              Each area splits its examples three ways — those carrying an <code>@intent</code> a
              human wrote, those SpecGuard read from the test's own description instead, and those it
              could not read at all — beside how many this run recorded there in total. Operands,
              never a percentage: an area at 40 of 40 unannotated is equally a module nobody has
              annotated yet and a module of generated specs nobody intends to, and nothing on this
              page decides which.
              <%# The distinction SPGD-711 exists to draw, said in the caption and not left to the
                  column headers. A reader who has been looking at this panel reading N of N for
                  every area needs to be told what changed, and the honest version of "derived" is
                  the sentence that says what it is missing rather than the one that sells it. %>
              A derived reading is real but weaker: it is inferred from prose somebody wrote for a
              test runner's output, it carries no preconditions, and its layer is guessed from the
              directory rather than declared. Only the third column is a count of tests SpecGuard can
              say nothing about.
              <%# An area with no debt is a ROW here, which surprises a reader who expected a list of
                  problems. Stated, because a `0` in a panel titled "where the unannotated tests are"
                  otherwise reads as a rendering bug. %>
              An area whose examples all carry an <code>@intent</code> is listed at 0 rather than
              dropped, so this is where the run's areas stand and not only where its debt is.
              <%# Counted off THIS RUN'S per-example rows. The Overview's figure is a subtraction over
                  the run's own counters, and while both sides read the same `status` the payload
                  wrote, the two populations are not guaranteed identical — a client that reports
                  totals for more examples than it sends detail for has more of the first than of the
                  second. So the sums here are not promised to add up to the headline, and saying so
                  is cheaper than a reader discovering it with a calculator. %>
              Counted off the per-example rows this run reported, which is a different population
              from the suite size on the Overview panel above and may legitimately differ from it.
            </p>

            <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Directory", "Carrying an @intent",
                                                        "Read from the description", "Not readable",
                                                        "Examples this run recorded"],
                                              describedby: "unannotated-directories-basis") do %>
              <% @unannotated_directories.rows.each do |directory| %>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">
                    <%# The way INTO the row. A count of an area's invisible tests is a finding a
                        reader cannot act on until they know WHICH tests, and until this link existed
                        there was no route to that anywhere on this page — the per-example worklist
                        was the API's, which the reader would have to leave the product and carry an
                        API key to reach.

                        `?spec_directory=`, the SAME ask "Heaviest spec directories" and "Areas that
                        grew or shrank" set. One ask opens EVERY panel that reads it, each answering
                        in its own grain over the same area, and `show.html.erb` states the rule at
                        "Areas that grew or shrank": that is how `drill_down_path` composes asks and
                        it is not a collision to be fixed by minting a second parameter.

                        Through `drill_down_path`, so every OTHER ask rides through — stated once in
                        the helper rather than re-decided in a hand-written argument here, which is
                        exactly how the sibling link at "Heaviest spec directories" came to drop an
                        open file for as long as it did.

                        `aria-current` on the open area, as both sibling links carry: this is a list
                        of choices with one of them taken, and the panel it opens is directly below
                        rather than in view. %>
                    <%= link_to directory.path,
                                drill_down_path(@repository, spec_directory: directory.path,
                                                anchor: "unannotated-examples"),
                                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                aria: { current: directory.path == @spec_directory_request ? "true" : nil } %>
                  </td>
                  <%# The operands, side by side and all bare integers. No fraction, no percentage,
                      no threshold and no colour on the row — `UnannotatedDirectories` states that
                      boundary twice and it governs this partial too. `tabular-nums` so a column of
                      counts lines up the way every other numeric column on this page does.

                      "Not readable" is the emphasised column and the one the ranking leads on, and
                      it is deliberately NOT the old "Not visible to SpecGuard" figure under a new
                      name: that one was `unannotated_count` and collapsed the middle column into
                      this one. `SpecObservation.unannotated_directories_in` argues the ordering. %>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                    <%= number_with_delimiter(directory.authored_count) %>
                  </td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                    <%= number_with_delimiter(directory.derived_count) %>
                  </td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content tabular-nums">
                    <%= number_with_delimiter(directory.unreadable_count) %>
                  </td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                    <%= number_with_delimiter(directory.recorded_count) %>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          <% else %>
            <%# The good news, said out loud, and scoped to exactly what these rows can see: every
                example this run RECORDED carries an intent. Not "the suite is fully annotated" — the
                Overview's subtraction is over the run's counters and answers that question for
                itself.

                `directory_count` and not `rows.size`: the count is taken before the LIMIT, so it
                covers the areas past the cap as well — and past the cap is where the head-of-list
                inference above is doing its work. %>
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "No unannotated tests in this run",
                  description: "Every example this run recorded carries an @intent, across all " \
                               "#{number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_directories.directory_count)} " \
                               "#{'directory'.pluralize(@unannotated_directories.directory_count)} " \
                               "it touched, so there is no area to rank here."
                ) %>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%# A run with no per-example rows AT ALL — ingested before those rows existed, or from a
              client that sends only totals. There is no per-area grain to disclose, and the one
              thing this state may not do is read as the state above it: "nobody sent the detail" and
              "nothing is unannotated" are the same empty table and opposite news.

              The panel stays rather than disappearing, because its absence would be a third
              indistinguishable thing on a page where the Overview may be printing a five-figure
              unreadable count at the same time — the reader would be told the debt exists and shown
              no reason this panel is not describing it. %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "No per-example detail on this run",
                description: "This run reported suite totals without the per-example rows SpecGuard " \
                             "rolls up by area, so there is nothing to say about where its " \
                             "unannotated tests are — not that it has none. Any count on the " \
                             "Overview above is taken from the run's own totals."
              ) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
